 - OMP's own per-message cost is used and marked **exact**. Claude Code costs are **estimated** from the price table (`.ta/pricing.toml`, then the built-in table); where a transcript splits cache writes by lifetime, the 1 hour part is priced at twice the input rate. A model with no price row is **unpriced**: its tokens are counted, its cost is 0 and nothing is invented. Add a row to `.ta/pricing.toml` to price it.
 - The context-size table groups turns by prompt size (input plus cache-read plus cache-creation tokens): 0 to 50k, 50 to 100k, 100 to 200k, 200 to 400k and over 400k. A long session that keeps re-reading a large context shows up as most of its cost in the top rows. TA goals are many turns each and are not in this table.
 
+### Exporting the cost report to Meridian
+
+To analyse cost against your KPIs in Meridian, write the report as a JSONL file Meridian's generic loader reads:
+
+```bash
+ta cost report --since 2026-10-01 --meridian ~/meridian/ta-cost.jsonl
+```
+
+One record is written per session or goal, with `id`, `title`, `timestamp`, `tokens_input` (uncached input), `tokens_output`, `source` and `phase` (goals only), plus string metadata `cost_usd`, `cost_exact` (`true` only when every turn's cost was reported by the runtime), `model`, `cache_read_tokens`, `cache_write_tokens`, `context_peak` (`n/a` for goals) and `workload` (`ta_goal` or `interactive`). The export covers exactly the rows the report totals, so `--since`, `--until` and `--project` apply to it, and repeated Claude message ids are counted once.
+
+The write is atomic (temp file, then rename) and idempotent by record `id`: run it again and records with the same id are replaced, others in the file are kept, nothing is duplicated. A session with no timestamp is skipped and counted. If the existing file has a line that is not valid JSON, TA refuses to rewrite it and tells you the line number. The command prints the path, the record count and how to read it: set `[source] jsonl = "<path>"` in `meridian.toml`, then run `meridian --config meridian.toml report`. With `--json`, that status goes to stderr so stdout stays parseable.
+
